Date: Tue, 26 Jan 1993 09:59:36 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Nutrition was: topics?

N.B.: a newsgroup sci.life-extension may be in the works.  Keep
glued, I'll try to forward the RFD to this list when I see it.

Eric Raymod:
>Even in
>our climate, I know people who tried going vegan and had to stop when they
>realized they were slowly starving to death.

These people may have felt hungry, and may have needed more clothes
in the winter to make up for the fat, but I heavily doubt they were
endangering their health due to lack of calories, much less starving.  
People at the bottom 10% of the weight/height ratio charts have the 
highest life expectancy in developed countries, after normalizing for 
those who have lost weight due to disease.  Dr. Roy Walford, among others,
advocates highly reduced calorie intake combined with acclimatizing
the body to cold temperatures (eg turning down the thermostat and
walking around the house in shorts).  This regimen extends the life
of lab rats up to 50%.

> Also, there are critical trace elements and some B vitamins that you 
>have to get either from animal products or supplements.  

The only significant nutrient in this regard seems to be vitamin B-12.
Life extenders should be taking supplements anyway; our food supply has
been so altered there is no garuntee that foods contain the vitamins or
minerals shown in the charts.  Still, it might be wise to eat two or 
three small servings of low-fat meat per week if it's accompanied by 
boatloads of plant fiber.

> Human beings are not really designed to be vegetarians --- our dentition,
> digestion, and dietary requirements are those of omnivores.

Most of us are omnivores, but our anatomy is that of a herbivore. Our 
"canines" are quite puny, even compared to 98%-herbivores like 
chimpanzees and gorillas.  Our saliva is alkaline and contains
the digestive enzyme ptyalin to break down starch; these are
characteristic of herbivores not carnivores.  Our stomach acids
are much weaker than what cats and dogs have for digesting
meat.  The carnivore's digestive track is usually three times the
length of the torso; herbivore's about ten times.  Homo sapien's
is twelve times.

Humans can of course eat mostly meat and survive past breeding age, 
but this is far from the norm, and is hardly optimal for life 
extension.  Studies of centenarians show a low meat and calorie
intake during their lifetime, compared to the mean.

> (Me?  Happy omnivore --- I live, effectively, on raw vegetables and (cooked)
> meat. 

This may be the opposite of optimal. Raw vegetables contain many 
natural pesticides which are highly carcinogenic, but can often 
be destroyed by boiling.  Cooked meat contains burned fats, which 
may be as carcinogenic to the gut as cigarette tar is to the lungs.
Cancer, for reasons I outlined previously, is by far the biggest
threat life extenders need to defend against.

For most people in the developed world the best simple longevity rule 
may be, the less you eat the better.

Nick Szabo					szabo@techbook.com

