Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 14:53:40 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON: Futures vs. Equities (was: Stupidity and natural selection)

My fundamental challenge is this: If you (Perry) say that futures is
"zero-sum", show me a set of relevant numbers that add up to zero.  I
don't doubt that you can do this, but I claim that I can probably find
a similar set of numbers for equities that add up to zero, so that
equities are no more zero-sum than futures.

>A futures contract is nothing but a bet on the future price of a
>commodity. ... I don't see that there is any way for the average
>return to be positive. 

Imagine I bet you my treasury bond to your treasury bond that my team
will win the Superbowl.  Our total average return, using a common
probability of winning for calculations, is just the return of the
treasury bond, minus transaction costs.  This can be positive.

Robin Hanson

