Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 18:36:38 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: PPL's and copyrights

> From: drw@bourbaki.mit.edu
> 
>    From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
> 
>    One idea which has been discussed for contractual enforcement of
>    copyright is to fingerprint each copy of a sold document.  Everyone
>    gets a slightly different copy.  Then if someone starts selling a bootleg
>    copy of the document, it may be possible to trace it to its original
>    source.
> 
> It would be quite hard.  Suppose you find out that the improper copies
> were made from Joe's copy.  You go to Joe and he says:  Someone stole
> it.  You don't have much of a case against Joe.

Well, one possible customary legal approach to this would be for the courts
to hold that anyone who steals a book to pirate it is getting "fruit of
a poison tree" and can be held accountable even if he hasn't signed an
explicit contract. The case against Joe then becomes immaterial.


Perry

