Back when I used to do conjuring as a hobby, I'd go to the library and find
many books "exposing" tricks.  Geller's key bending and mentalism are not
unique.

> expose of Gellers act. James Randi never had an original idea in his life 
> and is a second rate magician himself. He was only able to achieve fame
> at Gellers expense, and in a dishonorable way.

I think this insulting of Randi is factually wrong.  Randi's books, e.g.,
_Flim-Flam_, and activities such as the Alpha project[*] show him to be other
than a parasite.

[*] I may have the name wrong.  I refer to his having several young conjurers
    volunteer to participate in a study of psi powers.  I treasure my memory
    of a TV news report of one of the scientists involved (Was it at
    Georgetown U.?) saying that they had already proved such powers exist and
    that now they were just testing their extent.  The scientist were totally
    taken in until Randi called a press conference to expose the young
    conjurers.  One might question the ethics, but this project demonstrated
    yet again the skeptics' frequent observation that parapsychologists
    usually don't allow for deception.  (Of course, Eusapia Palladio (sp) was
    _really_ a psychic.  Those times she was shown to be faking were when she
    had to because her powers were erratic.  All the times the experiments
    were uncontrolled she was doing it for real.)

[...]
> treading on the territory of religion, but those of us who were close
> to the situation know that it was all Puharich. Geller rode the wave

How were you close to the situation?

[...]
> of publicity, but his aspirations were those of a performer, not a prophet.
[...]

His aspirations, perhaps.  However, the times I've seen him on TV or read
his quotes, he was claiming really to have psi powers rather than to be a
performer.  Anyone who claims such powers in a context where he is likely to
be believed deserves to be challenged.

[...]
